# class A { m() { this.x } }  class B { m() { this.x } }  class C { m() { this.x } }  function run(o) { o.m() }  run(new A()); run(new B()); run(new C())
# As many receivers at the call as the analysis tells apart: each method sees the instances of its own class alone. The pair with one receiver more than the limit is
# receivers-past-those-told-apart-reach-every-method.ir.
